<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cimmerians</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">8th–7th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Cimmerians were a nomadic people from the steppe north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> who invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> in the eighth and seventh centuries BCE; Assyrian records call them Gimirri. They overran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrygia">Phrygia</a>, killed the Lydian king Gyges in the mid-seventh century and raided the Greek cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionia">Ionia</a>, before being driven out by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydians">Lydians</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alyattes">Alyattes</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cimmerians.jpg" alt="Cimmerians" width="320" height="195" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Antiquistik · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cimmerian_Migrations.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Cimmerians were an Eastern Iranic equestrian nomadic people from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pontic-caspian-steppe">Pontic-Caspian steppe</a>. They were culturally Scythian and related to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a>, but ethnically distinct, and the Scythians later displaced and replaced them. The Cimmerians left no written records. Most information comes from Neo-Assyrian records of the 8th to 7th centuries BCE and from Greek and Roman authors from the 5th century BCE onward.</p><p>Their early homeland lay in the Caspian and Ciscaucasian steppes, north and west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspian-sea">Caspian Sea</a>, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> as the eastern border separating them from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerch-strait">Kerch Strait</a> at the west. The early Scythians began to arrive from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> in the 9th century BCE, driven west by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massagetae">Massagetae</a> or the Issedones.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the second half of the 8th century BCE and the 7th century BCE the Cimmerians moved south into West Asia, crossing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> by the Alagir, Darial and Klukhor passes. Mesopotamian sources call their first land in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-caucasus">Transcaucasia</a> māt Gamir, near the Kuros river, north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-sevan">Lake Sevan</a>. They later became active in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iranian-plateau">Iranian Plateau</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, and split into a western horde in Anatolia and an eastern horde that moved into Mannai and later Media.</p><p>Three kings are recorded: Teušpâ, who ruled until 679 BCE, Dugdammî, from 679 to 640 BCE, and Sandakšatru, from 640 to about the 630s BCE. The inroads of the Cimmerians and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a> upset the balance between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a>, Urartu and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrygia">Phrygia</a>, and contributed to the decline of several of them and the rise of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medes">Medes</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydians">Lydians</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Cimmerians were a tribal confederation, possibly ethnically mixed under an Iranic aristocracy, ruled by a supreme king whose power passed within a single dynasty. Military assemblies could be convened by the king, and warlords could rebel against him. They lived as mounted pastoralists like the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a>, with the same weapons, horse harness and Animal Style art.</p><p>The western Cimmerians in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> became settled, in fortified and other settlements, with a capital called Ḫarzallē. There they adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> warfare and infantry, and appear to have taken up the worship of the Anatolian god Šanta. Homer's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odyssey">Odyssey</a> gives the first mention of the Cimmerians in Greek literature, placing them in a misty land at the entrance of Hades.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cimmerians” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the seventh century BCE the <b>Cimmerians</b> raided western Asia Minor, and later tradition said they threatened the Artemision.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ephesus">Ephesus · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>From that century Scythians and <b>Cimmerians</b> migrating from Central Asia into the Pontic steppes invaded Colchis, and the Scythians made a base on the former territory of the kingdom from which they raided and campaigned in Media and Anatolia; Askold Ivanchik judges that Qulḫa, an independent flourishing state…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-colchis">Kingdom of Colchis · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Assyrian records call him Gugu of Luddi and report that he sought the help of Ashurbanipal against the <b>Cimmerians</b>, nomadic invaders from beyond the Caucasus who had destroyed the Kingdom of Phrygia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-lydia">Kingdom of Lydia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>According to Strabo and others, Midas killed himself by drinking bulls' blood when the <b>Cimmerians</b> overran his kingdom and sacked Gordium.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-phrygia">Kingdom of Phrygia · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>In 714 BCE Sargon II, informed by spies that Rusa I had just been defeated by the <b>Cimmerians</b>, marched round Lake Urmia, routed Rusa’s army in the mountains, devastated the country and sacked Musasir, carrying off the treasures of the temple of Haldi.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-urartu">Kingdom of Urartu · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The third account, which Herodotus himself preferred, held that the Scythians had been nomads in Asia who, pressed by the Massagetae, crossed the river Araxes and drove the <b>Cimmerians</b> from the steppe north of the Black Sea.</blockquote><a href="/culture/scythians">Scythians · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
